INT. MINICAB - MOVING - 2 A.M.

A tired Toyota Prius, smelling of pine air freshener and last Friday. Rain on the Westway. The driver, BARRY QUILL, 50s, cardigan, thermos in the cup holder, has the radio on low, playing something regrettable from 1987.

In the back, THE WOMAN, 30s, immaculate in a camel coat that has never seen a bus, looks out of the window and says nothing. Beside her sits a violin case, which Barry noticed and chose not to mention.

ON PHONE (her screen): "Two minutes out. Bring the case. Don't be clever."

                    BARRY
Dreadful night for it.

                    THE WOMAN
For what?

                    BARRY
Whatever you're doing. Orchestra, is it?

                    THE WOMAN
Something like that.

Her phone shows a second message: "Gate B. Quartet's in position." Barry drives on, serene as a vicar.

                    BARRY
I did a gig once. Trombone. Fell off the stage at a Rotary dinner. Terribly embarrassing.

                    THE WOMAN
(a very small smile)
I'm sure you recovered.

They turn into the gates of the Hatton Auction House, shuttered and dark. Four figures in black coats wait beneath the awning: THE QUARTET, holding what are almost certainly instruments.

                    THE QUARTET
(in unison, softly)
Evening, Mrs Vance.

                    THE WOMAN
(to Barry, handing over a fifty)
Keep it, Mr Quill.

                    BARRY
(frowning)
I don't recall telling you my surname.

She opens the violin case. It is empty, lined in velvet, exactly the size of a small painting.

                    BARRY (CONT'D)
Ah.

                    THE WOMAN
(opening the door)
Wait here, would you? There's a good chap.

She leaves. Barry looks at the thermos, at the rain, at the radio. Then, quite calmly, he turns the engine off.
